+{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_01AJNjSwo1NTpFdjzpeevkWr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Everything passes. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI built the versioned document store in `src/index.ts` (entry point) with a companion test suite in `src/index.test.ts`. All 28 tests pass, and `tsc` typecheck + `eslint` are clean.\n\n### Design\n\n- **Global version registry** — every `create`/`update`/applied-merge produces an immutable `VersionRecord` (`{id, seq, timestamp, content}`) keyed by a unique id (`v1`, `v2`, …). A monotonic `seq` gives reliable chronological ordering even when timestamps collide.\n- **Per-branch, per-document state** (`DocState`): a `head` pointer (null = deleted/absent), the document's own `history`, an `ancestors` set used for merge-base finding, and a `deleted` tombstone flag.\n- **Branching** copies each `DocState` (fresh array/set), so branches are fully isolated. Shared version ids in the `ancestors` sets are what let the merge recover the common ancestor.\n- **Deep cloning** via `structuredClone` on every store and retrieve, so external mutation can't leak in or out.\n\n### Three-way merge\n\nThe whole merge is a single recursive `mergeValue(base, ours, theirs)` applied per document (documents themselves are treated as values, with `undefined` meaning absent/deleted):\n\n1. `ours == theirs` → no change.\n2. `ours == base` → take theirs; `theirs == base` → take ours (one-sided change, no conflict).\n3. Both changed and both are plain objects → recurse key-by-key (this is the structural part — disjoint fields merge cleanly).\n4. Otherwise → record a conflict (with dot-path) and resolve in favour of *ours* for the `merged` preview.\n\nThis unification means add/add, delete/modify, document-level, and field-level cases all fall out of the same logic. Arrays are atomic. The **common ancestor** is the highest-`seq` version present in both branches' ancestor sets (`undefined` when there's no shared history, which correctly drives add/add merges).\n\nPer spec: no conflicts → auto-applied (`applied: true`, current branch updated, source history folded into ancestry so re-merges are idempotent); any conflict → nothing is applied and the current branch is left untouched, but `merged` still shows the would-be result.\n\nOne spec-ambiguous area I made a sensible call on: deletions. Delete-vs-unchanged propagates the deletion (no conflict); delete-vs-modify is reported as a conflict. Let me know if you'd prefer different deletion semantics."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":225,"cache_read_input_tokens":56161,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":225},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"de2a2552-de12-46a7-b956-f83395a02b47","uuid":"1896df19-0d7a-4382-b7d6-5062aeacc491","request_id":"req_011CbVc5aShHwaak9KeJfSeK"}
